import { COMPANY } from "../../../data/company";
---

<Layout
  title="Non-QM Loans: The Complete Guide | Cliffco Mortgage Bankers"
  description="What non-QM mortgages are, who they're for, and how to get one. Plain-English breakdown of bank statement, DSCR, P&L, 1099, and asset-based loans."
  type="article"
>
  <Breadcrumbs trail={[
    { name: "Home", href: "/" },
    { name: "Learn", href: "/learn/" },
    { name: "Non-QM Loans Guide", href: "/learn/non-qm-loans-guide/" },
  ]} />

  <section class="container-wide pt-12 pb-12">
    <p class="eyebrow">Non-QM</p>
    <h1 class="mt-3" style="font-size: var(--text-display-lg);">Non-QM Loans: The Complete Guide</h1>
    <p class="lead mt-6">
      Non-QM mortgages exist for borrowers who don't fit the standard mold. That includes the self-employed,
      real estate investors, and anyone whose income doesn't show up cleanly on a W-2.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="bg-white pb-12">
    <div class="container-prose">

      <h2>What Are Non-QM Loans?</h2>
      <p>
        "Non-QM" stands for non-qualified mortgage. It refers to any home loan that falls outside the
        Consumer Financial Protection Bureau's (CFPB) definition of a qualified mortgage. That might
        sound like a red flag, but it isn't. Non-QM simply means the loan doesn't follow a specific set
        of federal underwriting guidelines, not that the borrower is high-risk or unqualified.
      </p>
      <p>
        These loans exist because the standard qualified mortgage rulebook was designed around W-2 employees
        with predictable, documented income. A lot of creditworthy people don't fit that template. Business
        owners, freelancers, investors, retirees with assets but no paycheck, and foreign nationals often
        have the financial strength to repay a mortgage but can't prove it the way conventional guidelines
        require.
      </p>

      <h2>Types of Non-QM Loans</h2>
      <p>Non-QM is an umbrella. Under it, there are several distinct loan types, each designed for a
      different situation:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Bank statement loans</strong> — Income is verified using 12 to 24 months of personal or business bank statements instead of tax returns. Popular with self-employed borrowers whose tax returns show lower income after deductions.</li>
        <li><strong>DSCR loans</strong> — Short for Debt Service Coverage Ratio. The property's rental income qualifies the loan, not the borrower's personal income. Built for real estate investors.</li>
        <li><strong>Profit and Loss (P&amp;L) loans</strong> — Income is supported by a CPA-prepared P&amp;L statement rather than tax returns or statements.</li>
        <li><strong>1099 loans</strong> — Income is verified through 1099 forms, ideal for independent contractors and gig workers.</li>
        <li><strong>Asset depletion loans</strong> — Lender calculates a monthly income figure based on verified assets (retirement accounts, investment portfolios, savings). Good for high-net-worth borrowers who are asset-rich and income-light.</li>
        <li><strong>VOE loans</strong> — Verification of Employment only. No tax returns or pay stubs required, just employer verification of your salary or wages.</li>
        <li><strong>Interest-only loans</strong> — Payments cover only the interest for an initial period, which keeps monthly costs lower. Often used by investors with specific cash-flow strategies.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>How Does DSCR Fit In?</h2>
      <p>
        All DSCR loans are non-QM, but not all non-QM loans are DSCR. DSCR is one specific type within
        the non-QM category. If you're an investor focused on rental income, DSCR is typically the right
        conversation. If you're self-employed and buying a primary home, bank statement or P&amp;L is
        more likely the right tool.
      </p>

      <h2>What Property Types Are Eligible?</h2>
      <p>
        Non-QM loans are notably more flexible on property type than conventional loans. Eligible
        properties typically include primary residences, investment properties, mixed-use buildings,
        non-warrantable condominiums, co-ops, and properties in need of repair that wouldn't pass a
        standard appraisal.
      </p>

      <h2>What Are the Typical Requirements?</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Credit score:</strong> Most programs start at 620 to 680. Higher scores unlock better pricing.</li>
        <li><strong>Down payment:</strong> Generally 10% to 20%, depending on loan type and property.</li>
        <li><strong>Income documentation:</strong> Varies by loan type. Bank statements, 1099s, P&amp;L, or asset statements in place of W-2s and tax returns.</li>
        <li><strong>Reserves:</strong> Typically 3 to 12 months of payments held in liquid accounts after closing.</li>
        <li><strong>Debt-to-income:</strong> Many programs allow DTI up to 50%.</li>
        <li><strong>Loan terms:</strong> 15, 20, 25, and 30-year fixed options are common; some ARM products available.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Who Benefits Most From Non-QM?</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Self-employed business owners and sole proprietors</li>
        <li>Freelancers and independent contractors with irregular income</li>
        <li>Real estate investors building or managing portfolios</li>
        <li>Borrowers who experienced a past credit event (bankruptcy, foreclosure) and are past the waiting period</li>
        <li>High-net-worth individuals with significant assets but limited reportable income</li>
        <li>Foreign nationals purchasing U.S. property</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Where to Find Non-QM Loans</h2>
      <p>
        Non-QM lending is primarily done by non-bank mortgage companies, specialty lenders, credit unions,
        and private lenders. Big banks largely pulled back from this space after 2008. Cliffco has
        specialized in non-QM since before it had that name. It's one of our four core products, and our
        team originates these loans every day across 32 states.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="accent-gradient">
    <div class="container-wide py-16 text-center">
